1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,460
Ten of the biggest AI companies
in the world just changed how

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:04,940
they handle your data.

3
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:06,500
Here, in the UK.

4
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:07,140
And no...

5
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:09,650
they didn't just wake up feeling generous.

6
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:11,460
A regulator came knocking.

7
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:13,440
So what actually changed?

8
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:15,770
And is there anything you can do about it?

9
00:00:15,770 --> 00:00:16,820
Let's have a look.

10
00:00:16,860 --> 00:00:17,550
Okay.

11
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:20,250
Think about every review you've ever left.

12
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:23,990
Every public post, every
comment, every photo caption.

13
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:26,960
Now imagine each one is a postcard.

14
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:28,770
And the thing about postcards is...

15
00:00:28,770 --> 00:00:29,970
they're not sealed.

16
00:00:29,970 --> 00:00:32,100
Anyone who handles one can read it.

17
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:36,160
For years, AI companies have been
running a massive sorting office,

18
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:39,690
reading billions of those postcards
to learn how people talk.

19
00:00:39,690 --> 00:00:42,920
Here in the UK, the data
watchdog is the ICO.

20
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:46,200
And since last year, it's been
looking inside eleven of the biggest

21
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:47,490
sorting offices.

22
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:50,320
On the eighth of October,
it published what it found.

23
00:00:50,870 --> 00:00:51,900
And honestly?

24
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:53,600
It's a bit of a mixed bag.

25
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:54,630
First thing.

26
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:58,790
Some companies couldn't clearly
say why they needed your data.

27
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:03,410
And the ICO's view is basically:
a reason as vague as...

28
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:05,310
"benefiting humanity"?

29
00:01:05,310 --> 00:01:07,370
Yeah, that's not a reason.

30
00:01:07,370 --> 00:01:08,270
Second.

31
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:09,500
The small print.

32
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:11,680
The ICO called it a maze.

33
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:14,000
A notice here, a help page there.

34
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,770
And it says openness across the
industry has actually got worse.

35
00:01:17,770 --> 00:01:19,960
Third, and this is the big one.

36
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:23,110
Some of those postcards say
really personal things.

37
00:01:23,110 --> 00:01:23,960
Your health.

38
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:24,810
Your religion.

39
00:01:24,810 --> 00:01:25,910
How you vote.

40
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:29,450
And the ICO's view is: unless a
company can prove it hasn't got

41
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:30,680
that kind of data...

42
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:31,990
assume it has.

43
00:01:31,990 --> 00:01:33,830
So what did they change?

44
00:01:33,850 --> 00:01:37,160
Amazon, Anthropic,
Apple, Cohere, DeepSeek,

45
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:41,710
Google, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI
and Stability AI have all made

46
00:01:41,710 --> 00:01:43,700
changes, or promised to.

47
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:46,010
Clearer notices about what they train on.

48
00:01:46,010 --> 00:01:48,110
Easier ways to use your data rights.

49
00:01:48,110 --> 00:01:50,380
Tougher checks on their own safeguards.

50
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:52,630
And some of it is genuinely useful.

51
00:01:52,630 --> 00:01:55,980
OpenAI now explains how to ask
for your personal details to be

52
00:01:55,980 --> 00:01:58,440
taken out of ChatGPT's answers.

53
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:01,660
Meta has a form you can use even
if you've never touched Meta's

54
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:02,450
AI.

55
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:06,220
And Apple lets anyone flag a web
page to keep it out of training.

56
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:09,140
But here's the bit nobody's
really talking about.

57
00:02:09,170 --> 00:02:11,690
Once a postcard's been
read and learned from...

58
00:02:11,690 --> 00:02:13,640
you can't really un-read it.

59
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:17,060
The ICO says getting your data
back out of a trained model could

60
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:19,090
mean rebuilding the whole thing.

61
00:02:19,090 --> 00:02:22,220
And the regulator itself admits
the way these models are trained

62
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:25,300
makes UK data law
genuinely hard to follow.

63
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:27,450
It's taking that to the government.

64
00:02:27,450 --> 00:02:29,790
Oh, and one name's missing.

65
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:32,800
xAI, the company behind Grok.

66
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:34,260
It was on the list...

67
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:38,080
until the ICO opened a formal
investigation into it.

68
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:39,420
And what's next?

69
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:40,950
AI agents.

70
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:42,610
The ones that don't just chat.

71
00:02:42,610 --> 00:02:44,740
They actually do things for you.

72
00:02:44,810 --> 00:02:48,230
In tests this summer, some of them
reportedly slipped past their

73
00:02:48,230 --> 00:02:51,820
safety limits and reached systems
they weren't meant to touch.

74
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:53,390
The ICO's response?

75
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:57,770
Quote: "The fact AI agents act
with autonomy is not an excuse

76
00:02:57,770 --> 00:02:59,280
for poor compliance."

77
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:00,010
So.

78
00:03:00,010 --> 00:03:01,790
What can you actually do?

79
00:03:01,790 --> 00:03:02,950
3 things.

80
00:03:02,950 --> 00:03:03,670
One.

81
00:03:03,670 --> 00:03:05,730
Switch off training on your chats.

82
00:03:05,730 --> 00:03:09,360
In ChatGPT, that's
Settings, Data controls,

83
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:11,330
"Improve the model for everyone".

84
00:03:11,330 --> 00:03:16,190
In Claude, it's Settings, Privacy,
"Help improve our AI models".

85
00:03:16,190 --> 00:03:19,100
And in Gemini, turn off Keep Activity.

86
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:19,780
Two.

87
00:03:19,780 --> 00:03:22,040
If a chatbot says something
about you that's wrong,

88
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:25,180
or private, ask the company to remove it.

89
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:26,690
Those forms exist now.

90
00:03:26,690 --> 00:03:27,680
Use them.

91
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:28,700
And 3...

92
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:32,100
Treat anything you post
publicly like a postcard.

93
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:35,110
Because somewhere, something
might be reading it.

94
00:03:35,110 --> 00:03:38,180
Subscribe for more AI,
explained like this.
